An aerosol electrometer counts charge before it counts particles (@cm-aerosol-electrometer: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP", #source "../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE", #source "../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM", #clean-manufacturing "Swapping-instruments-can-separate-gain-from-splitter-bias.lingua#r_5XEWTJV7GW64WFADS6MRJBCCYG") { r_1BTK4C3JQ8MAC3YF6ZB2DZMG79
**Read:** [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), section 21.7.2; [CPC proportionality calibration — Owen, Mulholland and Guthrie](../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE), primary calibration; [Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues](../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM), counting-efficiency calibration.

For steady sample flow Q and complete collection of singly charged particles of one polarity, net current I − I₀ = eQC. Thus C = (I − I₀)/(eQ). Current, flow, background, collection and charge assumptions jointly establish the result. An accurate electrical current measurement alone does not establish an accurate aerosol number concentration.

For a population with charge fractions f_z and charge-dependent collection η_z, I − I₀ = eQC Σz z f_zη_z. Neutral particles contribute no current; opposite polarities can cancel. Treating mean signed charge as one can create a large number error. A classifier can simplify the charge population but does not remove the need to examine multiple-charge contamination.

**Example:** With singly charged particles, C = 1000 cm⁻³ and Q = 1 L/min, expected current is about 2.6702944 fA using e = 1.602176634×10⁻¹⁹ C exactly. If 10% of otherwise identical particles carry two charges and the remainder one, the current is 10% larger for the same particle number. Interpreting all as singly charged overestimates C by 10%.

**Work:** Derive both relations and calculate how an uncertain background current affects low concentrations. Compare that limitation with optical coincidence at high concentration. Explain why a calibration strategy may use different observations to establish the scale and the low-concentration response.

